; A proof whose derivation breaks at a specific step.
;
; `contra` needs a formula and its negation; here it is given one premise, so
; the checker gets stuck at that step rather than merely failing overall.
;
; Expected verdict: incorrect (see stuck-step.expected).
;
; What this covers is not the verdict -- no-refutation.proof already covers
; `incorrect` -- but the *diagnostic*. A checker that says only "incorrect"
; about a thousand-step proof has told you almost nothing. The replay in
; Cpc/Diagnostics.lean names the command:
;
;   the checker became stuck at step @p3 (proof command 2)
;
; The replay cannot disagree with the verdict: it is the same state machine run
; again, and nothing depends on its answer.

(declare-const x Int)
(declare-const y Int)
(assume @p0 (= y x))
(assume @p1 (not (= x y)))
(step @p2 :rule symm :premises (@p1))
(step @p3 :rule contra :premises (@p2))
